── no brokers
✗ kafka has no seed brokers

    kafka.Broker() was declared without kafka.Brokers(...).

  Give it at least one, usually from config:

      kafka.Broker(kafka.Brokers(cfg.Kafka.Brokers...))

  Seeds are only a starting point — the client discovers the rest of
  the cluster from them, so listing two or three is enough.

── no consumer group
✗ kafka has no consumer group

    A subscription to "user.registered" needs one, and kafka.Broker() was declared
    without kafka.ConsumerGroup(...).

  The group is what makes partitions divide between your replicas
  instead of every replica reading everything:

      kafka.Broker(
          kafka.Brokers(...),
          kafka.ConsumerGroup("billing-service"),
      )

  A service that only publishes needs no group.

── unknown balancer
✗ unknown partition assignment "nonesuch"

    kafka.PartitionAssignment("nonesuch")

  Available: cooperative (the default), sticky, roundrobin, range.

  Cooperative revokes only the partitions that actually move, so a
  rebalance does not stop every consumer in the group. Note it cannot
  coexist with the eager balancers in one group: changing this needs
  the whole group restarted, not a rolling deploy.

── plain without tls
✗ SASL/PLAIN without TLS

    kafka.SASL(kafka.Plain(...)) was set with no kafka.TLS(...).

  PLAIN sends the username and password in the CLEAR. This fails the
  boot rather than let a credential cross an unencrypted network
  because an option was forgotten.

  Add TLS:

      kafka.TLS(&tls.Config{MinVersion: tls.VersionTLS12})

  or use kafka.SCRAM512(...), which never sends the password itself.

── two mechanisms
✗ two SASL mechanisms

    Both kafka.SASL(...) and kafka.RawSASL(...) were set.

  Only one authenticates the connection, and which one would win is
  not something to leave to argument order. Keep kafka.SASL for the
  mechanisms Warren models — Plain, SCRAM256, SCRAM512 — and
  kafka.RawSASL only for the ones it does not, such as OAUTHBEARER
  or AWS MSK IAM.

── cannot connect
✗ cannot reach kafka

    a:9092, b:9092
    context deadline exceeded

  The seeds parsed, so this is the cluster, the network, or the
  credentials. A SASL failure looks the same as an unreachable broker
  from here — check both.

  This fails the boot deliberately: a service that cannot reach its
  broker cannot serve, and discovering that on the first publish is
  worse than discovering it now.

── raw failed
✗ kafka.Raw failed

    context deadline exceeded

  The client connected and the function passed to kafka.Raw returned
  an error, so the boot is abandoned and the client closed.

── not started
✗ kafka client used before it was started

    The client is opened by this module's OnStart hook, at boot step 6.

  Something published or subscribed during CONSTRUCTION instead. A
  constructor wires; it does not acquire. Move the call into an
  OnStart hook of your own, which runs after this one.
